# Kbuild fragment for the engine half of the .kpm.
#
# WHY THE ENGINE OBJECT IS BUILT BY KBUILD AND NOT BY OUR OWN COMPILER LINE
#
# It used to be compiled directly, with a hand-written K_FLAGS listing every
# -I the kernel headers need. That works against a normal in-tree checkout and
# breaks against a real GKI build tree, which is SPLIT: the generated headers
# (autoconf.h, include/generated) live in the build directory, the static ones
# (include/linux, the arch tree) live in a separate source tree, and the build
# directory reaches them through a `source` symlink. Reproducing that by hand
# means reproducing kbuild's include order, its -D flags, and its arch plumbing,
# and getting any of it subtly wrong changes struct layouts rather than failing
# the compile -- the worst possible failure for this particular object, which
# exists to dereference struct inode and dentry correctly.
#
# So: kbuild builds it, exactly as it builds any external module object, and the
# flags are whatever the target kernel actually uses.
#
# The ENTRY half is still compiled separately against the KernelPatch SDK
# headers -- see kpm/Makefile. The two are joined with `ld -r`.

obj-m += nm_engine.o

ccflags-y += -I$(src)/../hookless/src

# The engine is in-tree code being built somewhere it was not written for:
#   unused-function  -- vfs_map_meta_override() has no caller here
ccflags-y += -Wno-unused-function

# LTO AND CFI, and what turning them off costs.
#
# GKI 5.10 and 5.15 are built with ThinLTO, so kbuild emits LLVM bitcode rather
# than an ELF object, and the `ld -r` that assembles the .kpm rejects it:
# "file format not recognized". 6.1 and 6.6 linked fine, which is the sort of
# partial success that hides a problem until half the matrix goes red.
#
# A .kpm has to be a plain relocatable ELF object -- KernelPatch's loader
# relocates it itself -- so LTO cannot stay on. And on those kernels CFI rides
# on LTO: dropping one forces the other, or clang refuses with
# "invalid argument '-fsanitize=cfi' only allowed with '-flto'".
#
# WHAT THAT MEANS, stated plainly rather than left as a flag nobody reads:
# on a CONFIG_CFI_CLANG kernel this object's functions carry no CFI type
# identifiers. The engine installs function pointers into kernel structures
# (inode_operations, file_operations), and the kernel reaches them through
# indirect calls that CFI checks. An unidentified target is what CFI exists to
# stop, so this is a plausible panic on 5.10/5.15 and NOT something the build
# can prove either way -- no one has loaded this on hardware. It is recorded in
# kpm/README.md as the first thing to suspect if a load on those KMIs panics.
#
# The in-tree build has none of this problem: it is compiled with the kernel,
# LTO, CFI and all. That remains the supported way to run the engine.
ccflags-y += -fno-lto
ccflags-y += -fno-sanitize=cfi
